/rename now respects the [directories] deny list that /cd applies. Before this, it could still rewrite files in a directory the new project's .yoyo.toml had denied. Build, all tests, clippy and fmt pass. It's committed as 1574d3ae, with "Part of #869" in the title.

The cause: partition_denied_files in src/commands_rename.rs only checked the deny list set at startup. It also had a shortcut: with no startup restrictions, it kept every file. So after a /cd with no startup deny list, nothing was ever checked against the /cd list.

The fix: it now uses path_guard_refusal_with(cd_dir_deny_list(), restrictions, f), the same check the file tools use: startup list first, then the /cd list. The shortcut only applies when both lists are empty. The model's rename_symbol tool calls the same function, so it gets the fix too.

Tests (in commands_rename.rs, using temp dirs and a per-thread test list rather than the real global one):
• With no startup deny list, a file under a /cd-denied directory is left untouched and reported as skipped.
• With an empty `/cd list, or one naming an unrelated directory, the set of files touched is exactly the same as before (checked with assert_eq!` on the full result).
• A startup `--deny-dir` still excludes files on its own.
• **Sabotage check:** I temporarily put back the old check, and the new deny test failed by name; restored, everything passed again. No sabotage marker was left in the code.

Register: I removed the commands_rename.rs row from UNWIRED_CHECK_PATH_READERS. The source guard is green with 3 rows left: commands_project.rs, context.rs, skill_fence.rs.

Which remaining reader still matters: I checked rather than took the reflection comment's word for it. commands_project.rs is the only one that can run after /cd, because its auto-context runs on every REPL prompt (repl.rs:1215 calls auto_context_for_prompt). context.rs and skill_fence.rs only run at startup.

Docs and issue: I added one entry to ARCHITECTURE.md's cd_config_note.rs section and posted the new count and the remaining reader on #869.

